## Refactored: Callbacks → `async/await`

```js
// ─── Single fetch ───────────────────────────────────────────────
async function load(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);

  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} – ${res.statusText} (${url})`);
  }

  try {
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to parse JSON from ${url}: ${err.message}`);
  }
}

// ─── Parallel fetch (ordered results) ──────────────────────────
async function all(urls) {
  if (!Array.isArray(urls) || urls.length === 0) {
    throw new TypeError("all() expects a non-empty array of URLs");
  }

  // Promise.all preserves index order and rejects on first failure,
  // matching the original "fail-fast" behaviour.
  return Promise.all(urls.map((url) => load(url)));
}
```

### Usage

```js
try {
  const [user, posts] = await all([
    "https://api.example.com/user",
    "https://api.example.com/posts",
  ]);
  console.log(user, posts);
} catch (err) {
  console.error("Batch load failed:", err.message);
}
```

### What changed and why

| Concern | Original | Refactored |
|---|---|---|
| **Control flow** | Nested `.then` / callback `(err, data)` | `async / await` – linear, readable |
| **HTTP errors** | Silently treated as "success" (no `res.ok` check) | Explicit `!res.ok` → `throw` |
| **JSON parse errors** | Swallowed into the generic `catch` | Separate `try/c
